meta {
  name: Invite User
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/users/invite
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

script:pre-request {
  // A fresh address every run. `User.email` is unique and this endpoint answers
  // a repeat with 409 by design, so a hard-coded email would pass once and then
  // fail on every subsequent `bru run`. **Revoke Invite** deletes this row again
  // at the end of the folder, so the collection leaves no residue either way.
  bru.setVar("inviteEmail", `pac58-invite-${Date.now()}@sfa.local`);
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "{{inviteEmail}}",
    "roleIds": ["{{inviteRoleId}}"],
    "branchId": "{{inviteBranchId}}",
    "firstName": "Pending",
    "lastName": "Invitee"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 201) {
    const body = res.getBody();
    bru.setVar("invitedUserId", body.userId);
    // Dev/test only — absent in production, where the email is the only
    // delivery channel. See the docs block.
    bru.setVar("inviteToken", body.inviteToken);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("the invite URL is absolute", function () {
    // Regression guard: this used to return the relative
    // `/auth/accept-invite?token=…`, which is unusable from an email client.
    expect(res.getBody().inviteUrl).to.match(/^https?:\/\//);
    expect(res.getBody().inviteUrl).to.contain("/auth/accept-invite?token=");
  });

  test("expires in the future", function () {
    expect(new Date(res.getBody().expiresAt).getTime()).to.be.greaterThan(Date.now());
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /users/invite

  Create an inactive user, mint a 32-byte invite token, and email them a link to
  set their own password.

  **Module** — none (`@SkipModule`). **Permission** — `agency:users:write`
  (Agency Owner). A producer or CSR gets `403`.

  **Body**
  | field     | type     | rules                                        |
  |-----------|----------|----------------------------------------------|
  | email     | string   | valid email; trimmed + lowercased server-side |
  | roleIds   | string[] | non-empty; each a valid id belonging to the agency |
  | branchId  | string?  | a branch **of this agency**; omit to leave unplaced |
  | firstName | string?  | ≤ 100 chars                                   |
  | lastName  | string?  | ≤ 100 chars                                   |

  The UI assigns exactly one role, but the contract stays an array to match
  `PATCH /users/:userId/roles`.

  **Response `201`**
  ```json
  {
    "userId": "...",
    "inviteUrl": "http://localhost:5173/auth/accept-invite?token=<hex>",
    "expiresAt": "2026-08-24T09:00:00.000Z",
    "inviteToken": "<hex>"
  }
  ```

  > ⚠ `inviteToken` is returned **only when `NODE_ENV` is not `production`**.
  > It is a bearer credential and the email is its delivery channel. It is
  > exposed outside production purely because delivery is still a stub — the API
  > logs the rendered email instead of sending it (see `MailService`), so this is
  > the only way to walk the flow locally. It disappears when a real transport
  > lands.

  **Errors**
  - `400` — no roles, a role outside the agency, a branch outside the agency, or
    a malformed email/id. The branch check matters: `branchId` is only shape-
    validated by the DTO, so without it a new employee could be placed in
    another tenant's branch and stay there.
  - `403` — caller lacks `agency:users:write`.
  - `409` — the address is taken. The message distinguishes **already a member**
    from **already has a pending invite**, because the owner's next action
    differs (nothing vs. resend).

  ## Chaining
  `inviteRoleId` comes from **List Roles (for the invite picker)** and
  `inviteBranchId` from **List Branches (for the invite picker)**, both of which
  run first. The email is generated per run in the pre-request script, and
  **Revoke Invite** deletes the row at the end of the folder — so a repeated
  `bru run` neither collides on the unique `email` index nor accumulates
  half-invited users.
}
